distroforge (0.3.5-16) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Released versions are anchored to commits. This changelog reached 46
    versions while the repository held no tag at all, local or remote, and
    no GitHub release: a changelog says a version existed, only a tag says
    where. debian/gbp.conf now pins DEP-14's debian/<version> naming and
    `make tag` cuts one through tools/release-tag.sh. Publishing the tag
    stays a separate command, since a pushed tag must not be moved after.
  * Fixed two dead settings in debian/gbp.conf, the file that governs all
    of the above. upstream-branch named "upstream" from the 0.3.5-1
    baseline import onward -- a branch that has never existed here, since
    upstream and the packaging are one tree -- and upstream-tree was left
    at gbp's default of TAG, which sends export-orig looking for an
    upstream/<version> tag this workflow never creates. distroforge doctor
    advertises gbp as the release workflow, so neither was harmless; both
    survived because nothing had ever run it.
  * Pinned sign-tags = True, which is not the redundancy it looks like:
    gbp/git/repository.py passes --no-sign when that option is unset,
    overriding tag.gpgsign = true in the repository config. Unpinned, the
    first release tag of this project would have been unsigned, sitting
    over 53 commits that are every one of them signed.
  * Three tests hold it. One resolves the branches gbp.conf names against
    real refs, which is what would have caught the dead one. One requires
    every debian/* tag to be annotated, to carry a signature block, and to
    name the version that its own commit's changelog declares, read from
    the tagged tree rather than the checkout. One asserts the target, the
    script and the document are still joined, the same shape of guard as
    the key-export procedure. packaging-static now checks out with
    fetch-depth: 0, because actions/checkout fetches no tags: the tag gate
    would have passed on an empty list and reported itself green.
  * Protected main, develop and refs/tags/debian/* with two repository
    rulesets and no bypass actors, so they bind the owner, who is the only
    person who pushes. Branches: deletion, non_fast_forward,
    required_linear_history, required_signatures. Tags additionally take
    update, without which a tag could still be moved forward onto a
    descendant. required_signatures is deliberately not on the tags: the
    documentation describes it only for commits on a branch, and a rule
    whose effect cannot be verified reads like protection without being
    it, so the tag's signature is asserted by the test instead.
  * Recorded why a pull-request flow and required status checks are
    excluded rather than leaving it to look like an omission. GitHub has
    no fast-forward merge -- the method enum is closed at merge, squash
    and rebase, and rebase is documented to always write new SHAs and to
    drop the signatures -- so routing main through a pull request would
    trade an identical signed commit for a rewritten unsigned one, which
    is the property this project keeps. Required checks are evaluated
    against the commit being pushed, which has no results yet, so they
    forbid direct pushes and force that same flow.
  * Corrected two files that asserted the opposite of the above.
    debian/watch said "there is no release tag" and promised a mode=git
    rule once tags existed; both were wrong, since packaging tags on this
    same tree give uscan nothing to poll but this package itself.
    debian/README.source repeated the claim. A test docstring still cited
    Policy 5.6.2 for the maintainer address 130 lines below the test that
    says the rule is 3.3, left behind when that was corrected in 0.3.5-11.
  * tools/*.sh is now swept by both shellcheck gates, the Makefile target
    and the pre-commit hook, so a release script cannot ship unchecked the
    way debian/tests/gui-import nearly did.
  * The tests above shell out to git, which the distro-dependencies job
    does not have: it installs distribution packages only, so
    actions/checkout falls back to the REST tarball and that leg runs with
    no git binary and no .git at all. The first version raised
    FileNotFoundError there and turned "no history to check" into a red
    job. The helper now reports a missing git the way it reports a failed
    git call, and the tests skip rather than crash.

