distroforge (0.3.5-14) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Keep a personal mailbox out of anything this project publishes. The signing
    key carries two user ids, one of them a personal address, and a full
    `gpg --export` writes both into whatever keyring it produces. Audited every
    site that could carry a key: nothing in this tool exports a public key at
    all. Signing produces detached signatures, which carry a key id and no user
    ids; the only keyring written into an image belongs to a third party,
    fetched from a keyserver by fingerprint in core/ppa.py. No code to fix,
    and the exposure lives entirely in the procedure for publishing an apt
    repository, which was not written down anywhere.
  * docs/packaging-release.md now carries it, with the filter measured rather
    than assumed: the whole key exports as 2927 bytes with both user ids, the
    filtered form as 2278 with one, and the filtered export imported into an
    empty GNUPGHOME verifies a real signature made by that key -- verification
    uses the key material, not the labels on it. Also recorded there: the filter
    governs one export and not a key already uploaded somewhere, and
    keys.openpgp.org and keyserver.ubuntu.com both answer 404 for this
    fingerprint, so nothing personal is published yet.
  * Two guards, since the export is a procedure and not a function. One rejects
    any mailbox in the tree that is not a published identity -- an allowlist on
    purpose, because a denylist would have to spell out the address it protects,
    in a public repository. The other rejects a documented `gpg --export` that
    carries no `--export-filter`, and fails if the procedure is deleted. Both
    verified to fail on an injected violation.
  * .mypy_cache joins SKIP_DIRS. `make typecheck` creates it on every developer
    machine and its JSON holds dotted symbol names that read as mail addresses;
    no test had ever looked inside it before this one did.

