distroforge (0.3.5-12) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Stop dating entries in a future that had not happened yet. Comparing every
    trailer with the author date of the commit that introduced its line found
    four in a row ahead of their own commit: 0.3.5-7 by 3 minutes, 0.3.5-8 by
    13, 0.3.5-9 by 146 and 0.3.5-10 by 160 -- the last two still ahead of the
    wall clock two hours later. dpkg-parsechangelog hands that field to the
    archive as the release date of the version, so the package was claiming to
    have been released two and a half hours after it was built. The four now
    carry the author dates of their commits. Entries at 0.3.5-2 and below
    predate this git repository and are the original packaging record; they are
    left exactly as they were.
  * Why nothing caught it, which is the part worth keeping. lintian's changelog
    check reads the newest entry's weekday, a short list of textually invalid
    dates, and whether the newest entry is newer than the one directly below it.
    There is no future-date tag at all, and nothing under the top two entries is
    examined.
  * The new check reads the whole file rather than its top: every trailer parses
    as RFC 5322, declares the weekday its own date really fell on, is strictly
    newer than the entry below it, and is not in the future. It runs from the
    changelog-policy pre-commit hook, so it fires while a wrong date can still
    be corrected for free -- which is the only moment the future-date half can
    work, since once the clock passes a fabricated date it is no longer in the
    future. Verified to fail on the file as it stood.

