distroforge (0.3.5-10) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Give the package a maintainer address that can receive mail. Every entry so
    far named maintainers@distroforge.invalid, an RFC 2606 reserved domain that
    is guaranteed never to resolve, which makes the package unuploadable under
    Debian Policy 5.6.2 whatever else is clean about it. The address is now a
    forwarding alias, so it is public and deliverable without exposing a
    personal mailbox, and the role phrase is unchanged.
  * Substituted at the three sites that reach anyone: debian/control,
    debian/copyright (Upstream-Contact) and _DEB_MAINTAINER in
    core/desktop_source.py, which stamps it into the DEBIAN/control of every
    desktop component .deb the tool builds for a user. Test fixtures in
    test_gpg_signer_pinning.py and test_packaging_reports.py deliberately keep
    a .invalid address -- that is what RFC 2606 reserves it for -- and the
    changelog entries above are history and are not rewritten.
  * Stop pointing the SPDX documentNamespace at a domain that cannot exist.
    SPDX 2.3 needs a unique absolute URI and does not require it to resolve,
    which is how .invalid survived there, but an SBOM shipped beside a package
    should name a namespace the project actually controls. It now uses the
    repository URL rather than the alias domain, which belongs to the
    forwarding provider and not to this project.
  * Guard the class, not the spelling: the new check rejects any maintainer
    address in an RFC 2606 reserved TLD and any GitHub noreply, the two
    plausible wrong answers. Verified to fail on both. It cannot prove an
    address really delivers -- that needs mail actually sent to it, which an
    offline suite cannot do -- so it rules out what is provably undeliverable
    and leaves the live check to the manual step.

