distroforge (0.3.5-8) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Give the nspawn maintainer shell a resolver, and stop it writing into the
    image. Decided by measurement rather than by reading the manual page:
    2026-07-27, ten --resolv-conf modes and four flag sets, each against a
    throwaway rootfs carrying the shape a real one has -- an /etc/resolv.conf
    symlink into ../run, an /etc/localtime symlink, an empty /etc/machine-id --
    with the whole tree diffed afterwards. The default, auto, left the shell
    unable to resolve anything: the copy modes skip a destination that is not a
    regular file, and that symlink is what systemd-resolved's postinst leaves,
    so copy-host and copy-stub declined and apt in the shell failed on
    "Temporary failure resolving". The replace modes resolve by writing the
    build machine's nameserver into the image over that symlink, which is the
    leak taken out of the build phases in 0.3.5-4. bind-host resolves --
    archive.ubuntu.com answered from inside -- and leaves the image untouched,
    because the mount dies with the container.
  * Stop every session leaving /var/log/journal in the rootfs. nspawn's default
    for --directory is --link-journal=try-guest, which creates it in the image;
    docs/architecture.md says this backend must not mutate hermetic build
    chroots, and it had been doing so since it shipped. With --link-journal=no
    the tree comes out byte-identical.
  * Make the shell read the image's clock instead of the build machine's. The
    default --timezone=auto binds the host's zone over the image's, so date
    printed CEST inside an image whose own /etc/localtime says UTC. The symlink
    survives either way, so this is about what the operator sees, not about
    contamination: --timezone=off.
  * One difference from the chroot backend is accepted rather than hidden: a
    bind hands over the host's whole resolver file, search domain included,
    where host_resolver_content() keeps only the nameserver lines. Nothing of it
    survives the session, and the alternative is plumbing a filtered copy
    through a lifetime ChrootTerminalSpec does not have.

