distroforge (0.3.5-7) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Let the automated QEMU proofs use the hardware acceleration the host
    already offers. The lab, the boot-check, the QA matrix and the screenshot
    capture all ran under TCG, QEMU's interpreter, because the flag was
    reachable only from the interactive preview: five launch sites said nothing
    and took the dataclass default. They now share one probe, kvm_is_usable(),
    which asks whether /dev/kvm opens rather than whether it exists -- on this
    build machine it is root:clock mode 0660 with a POSIX ACL granting the
    developer rw, so neither the owning group nor the caller's group list
    answers the question and access(2) does, ACLs included. Measured
    2026-07-27, booting one desktop kernel and its 88 MB initramfs from the
    same media under the lab's own defaults: 7.5 s to the end of the initramfs
    emulated against 4.5 s accelerated, with the Secure Boot firmware emitting
    the same 217 bytes either way. -cpu host was measured too and is
    deliberately not passed: it bought nothing here (5.0 s), and leaving it out
    keeps the guest CPU model identical with and without acceleration, so a
    proof from an accelerated host stays comparable to one from a runner that
    has to emulate.
  * Stop the interaction lab dying on a host that cannot accelerate. Its
    option defaults to True and was passed through unchecked, so QEMU exited on
    "Could not access KVM kernel module" rather than falling back to the
    emulation that would have worked. The preview's own check went the other
    way and was two wrong questions -- Path("/dev/kvm").exists() or
    has_binary("kvm") -- since a device node can exist and refuse to open, and
    that wrapper ships with qemu-system-x86 on hosts with no virtualisation at
    all.
  * Record the answer in qemu-lab-report.json as accelerated. Two runs of the
    same command on the same ISO differ by more than half their wall-clock
    depending on it, and until it was written down a reader had no way to tell
    which of the two they were holding, nor whether a run that exhausted its
    timeout had been emulating the whole way.
  * Keep the host out of the suite's answer. A session fixture aims the probe
    at a path that cannot exist, so no test asserts one argv on a developer's
    machine and a different one on a runner, and a walk over every
    QemuInvocation( call in the package keeps a seventh launch site from
    quietly going back to emulation. The install smoke planner is the one
    deliberate exception: it writes command lines into a report for someone to
    run later, possibly on another machine, so the planning host's acceleration
    has no business in them.
  * Correct the 0.3.5-6 timestamp, which was two hours ahead of the commit
    that wrote it and would have dated this entry before it.

